steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;EM&bull;Scope Capture Collections}

      So far, we've worked with pre-populated collections of **EM&bull;Scope** captures.&thinsp; Now you'll create your own&thinsp;!!

      Any EMS **bucket** can hold _one_ capture collection (named `emscope-captures`), alongside related source files.

      Our familiar {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} captures {[dc,1]} reside within `em.benchmark.coremark` &ndash; as does {[cu]SleepyRunnerP} itself.

      Individual `em.mark.*` _activity_ buckets (such as {[dc,2]} and {[dc,3]}) follow this same pattern.

      Clicking on any `emscope-captures` element {[dc,1]} opens this capture collection within **EM&bull;Scope**.

      <div class="em-info">

      This tour uses the special `zlocal` package as a private sandbox for experimenting with **EM&bull;Scope**.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$build]&thinsp;Prepare the Benchmark}

      The capture collection you'll soon create will benchmark al&shy;ternative forms of "waiting" on a particular MCU.

      <div class="em-info">

      This tour chose the `ti.cc23xx` distro for its examples &ndash; which work equally well with other supported MCUs.

      </div>

      Our first benchmark uses the {[cu]BusyWaitA} activity from the prior tour {[tr,104/03/03]} &ndash; to wait for 50&thinsp;ms, once per second.

      We've copied {[cu]BusyWaitC} {[tr,104/03/06]} into our own compos&shy;ite [{[cu]BusyWait50C}] which explicitly binds {[cn]duration_us} {[dc,1]}.

      Running {[cu]BusyWait50C}, **Logic 2** confirms the program's ten periodic events {[dc,2]} which each spend ~50&thinsp;ms {[dc,3]} waiting.

      <div class="em-info">

      To "play along at home", create your _own_ private copy of `BusyWait50C.em.ts` within the `zlocal` package.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - view 3
    text: |
      {[ht,$look]&thinsp;Connect and Verify}

      With {[cu]BusyWait50C} built and loaded, we'll now observe its _current consumption_ using Nordic's [PPK2](https://www.nordicsemi.com/Products/Development-hardware/Power-Profiler-Kit-2) analyzer.

      <div class="em-info">

      The **PPK** operates as a **Source** or **Ampere** meter &ndash; the form&shy;er powering the DUT at a selected voltage.

      </div>

      In this setup, we've disconnected the debugger adapter from the DUT and then connected the **PPK** as shown {[dc,1]}.

      Using Nordic's desktop app, select  **Source Meter** at 3V0 and start recording _before_ you enable power to the DUT.

      A trace like {[dc,2]} should appear, with a large inrush at reset followed by ten WORK-SLEEP activity cycles.

      Zooming in to one WORK burst {[dc,3]} shows sustained cur&shy;rent during busy-wait, followed by a return to deep SLEEP.

  - cmds:
      - view 4
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;Create a Collection}

      Examples in this tour use the `my.bench.wait` **Bucket**, which you should place within the `zlocal` **Package**.

      The right-click context menu of `my.bench.wait` enables us to create a new collection of **EM&bull;Scope** captures {[dc,1]}.

      Upon creation, the `emscope-captures` element {[dc,2]} ap&shy;pears in the bucket &ndash; alongside related source code.

      <div class="em-info">

      The **EM&bull;Scope** view simultaneously opens, with explicit linkage back to {[cu]zlocal://my.bench.wait}.

      </div>

      We've increased the scan event window to 300&thinsp;ms {[dc,3]} for our planned workloads, using features seen at&thinsp; {[tr,104/02/05]}.

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;Add a Capture}

      Adding a new capture to a collection entails five steps:

      {[DC,1]} click the **`+`** button to launch the **New Capture** dialog<br>
      {[DC,2]} supply a name, review the options, then click accept<br>
      {[DC,3]} monitor the status bar for {[em]EM&bull;Scope} progress<br>
      {[DC,4]} review the provisional capture, then accept/reject it<br>
      {[DC,5]} the new capture finally joins this collection<br>

      Before clicking {[dc,1]}:&nbsp; build/load the benchmark program, connect the **PPK** to the DUT, and _quit_ the Nordic app.

      <div class="em-info">

      As a check, the pre-populated **New Capture** fields reflect properties of the _latest_ EMS build.

      </div>

      Before choosing accept/reject at {[dc,4]}, you can _view_ {[tr,104/02/02]} or _re-scan_ {[tr,104/02/05]} data in the provisional capture.

  - cmds:
      - view 6
    text: |
      {[ht,$build]&thinsp;Vary the Workload}

      We've copied {[cu]BusyWait50C} into a new **Composite** which increases {[cu]BusyWaitA}.{[cn]duration_us} to `250000` {[dc,1]}.

      To verify correctness, we build/load {[cu]BusyWait250C} and use **Logic 2** to inspect the timing of program execution.

      <div class="em-info">

      The trace at {[dc,2]} shows the expected ten WORK-SLEEP cycles; the zoom at {[dc,3]} confirms ~250&thinsp;ms of activity.

      </div>

      Using the five-step process outlined at the last stop, we've added a `wait-250` entry {[dc,4]} to our capture collection.

      Consuming ~5&times; the WORK energy, {[cu]BusyWait250C} earns roughly one-fifth the {[em]EM&bull;eralds} score &ndash; as anticipated.

  - cmds:
      - view 7
    text: |
      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;An Alternative Activity}

      Our next benchmark returns to the original workload of "waiting" for 50&thinsp;ms &ndash; but now uses an alternative activity.

      The {[cu]Pause50C} **Composite** imports the stock {[cu]PauseA} {[dc,1]} and explicitly binds its {[cn]duration_us} config to `50000` {[dc,2]}.

      The {[cu]PauseA} activity then binds {[cu]BoardC}.{[cn]OneShot} to the {[cu]Poller}.{[cn]OneShot} proxy {[dc,3]} &ndash; waiting with a _light pause_.

      The **Logic 2** traces at {[dc,4]} and {[dc,5]} confirm ten WORK-SLEEP event cycles and ~50&thinsp;ms of WORK duration.

      <div class="em-info">

      Looking carefully at `DbgB`, however, reveals that the CPU in fact lightly idles during this interval&thinsp; {[tr,101/06/04]}.

      </div>

      Once entered in the collection {[dc,6]}, `pause-50` more than _doubles_ the **EM&bull;eralds** efficiency scores of `wait-50`.

  - cmds:
      - view 8
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;Manage a Capture Collection}

      **EM&bull;Scope** offers a number of features for managing entries in a capture collection:

      {[DC,1]} reveal its meta-data by hovering over its name<br>
      {[DC,2]} **Edit** meta-data or **Grab Again** to record new signals<br>
      {[DC,3]} **Remove** this entry from the collection<br>

      <div class="em-info">

      You'll find features {[dc,2]} and {[dc,3]} on the right-click context menu associated with a particular capture entry.

      </div>

      To remove the _entire_ collection, use **Remove** {[dc,4]} from the `emscope-captures` right-click menu {[tr,100/02/06]}&thinsp;.

  - cmds:
      - view 9
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Create {[cu]Pause250C} and compare with `wait-250`.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Compare **FLASH**&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;**SRAM** versions of one benchmark.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Create a new capture collection with your own activity.

  - cmds:
      - view 10
    text: |
      {[ht,school]&thinsp;Congratulations}

      You've completed the **EM&bull;Tours 100** curriculum &ndash; focusing first on *code size* and culminating with *energy efficiency*:

      {[DC,1]} we built compact programs, many smaller than 2&thinsp;KB<br>
      {[DC,2]} we used logic traces to quantify execution timing<br>
      {[DC,3]} we examined power captures to measure energy<br>
      {[DC,4]} we combined WORK&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;SLEEP into **EM&bull;eralds** scores<br>

      You now have the tools to build, measure, and improve your _own_ {[em]Tiny code for Tiny chips}.

      {[hc]}
